      "Microstructure_Info": {
        "Microstructure_Text": {
          "original": "The effect of grain coarsening after HIP is visually evident in the backscatter and EBSD images. Based on approximations derived from EBSD data in vertically built 90° specimens before and after HIP, using equivalent ellipse fitting method, after HIP, the lath length and breadth increase by approximately two times. Approximate average lath statistics for specimens manufactured at 0°, 30° and 90° after HIP show that for all angles the average length and breadth is larger in the bulk region by less than 7 %, the average lath area is larger in the bulk region by less than 19 %, average lath length for 30° specimens is less than for 0° and 90° in both bulk and edge regions, and average lath breadth for 30° specimens is greater than 0° and 90° specimens in the bulk region. In regions close to the side and downward-facing surfaces, columnar growth of prior-beta grains parallel to build direction is not obvious. However, clear bands of similarly oriented laths can be seen within columnar prior beta grains in the bulk region.",
          "simplified": "HIP causes lath coarsening in LB-PBF Ti-6Al-4V. In 90° specimens, lath area, length, breadth and aspect ratio all increase after HIP. After HIP, bulk regions have slightly larger lath size than edge regions, while orientation-related differences are modest. Columnar prior-beta growth is less obvious near side and downward-facing surfaces than in the bulk."
        },
